            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Before the new one: say the Latin for play, then the Latin for to wrestle."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know: pūpa, pūpae",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "pūpa, pūpae — \"a doll; a little girl\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Fīliola cum pūpā suā dormit. The little daughter sleeps with her doll."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Feminine, first declension. Before her wedding a Roman girl gave her dolls to the household gods."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "pūpa is the feminine of pūpus \"boy, child\" (Lewis & Short). English puppet comes from it, and pupil from the diminutive pūpillus."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "notice",
